From 2702883ad71f95ec169013b5da418a106ab4ec28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:49:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/14] build(gateway): build and test with Go 1.27

The gateway image and the CI go job used Go 1.22, which has been out
of support since February 2025. The gateway depends only on the
standard library, which is compiled into the binary, so its security
fixes arrive only with Go patch releases: govulncheck reports 41
vulnerabilities in standard library code the gateway calls (net/http,
crypto/tls, crypto/x509, net/url and others) with go1.22.12, the last
1.22 release, and none with go1.26.9 or go1.27.2.

Build the image from golang:1.27 and run the go job with the same
minor version, read from a workflow-level GO_VERSION so that jobs
cannot drift apart; both take the latest patch release.

Raise the go line in go.mod from 1.22 to 1.27.0 too: it selects the
GODEBUG defaults, and a Go 1.27 build of a "go 1.22" module keeps the
old behaviour of every setting changed since. For the gateway that
meant no limit on the number of query parameters the reverse proxy
parses, a re-encoded and therefore reordered query on every request
forwarded to the control plane (TestAPIKeyMode_ForwardsAuditEventsQuery
failed on Go 1.27), and a GOMAXPROCS that ignores the container's CPU
limit. Now a query is forwarded as sent unless it has more than 10000
parameters, in which case it is dropped; GOMAXPROCS follows the CPU
limit, rounded up to at least 2; and the listener accepts Multipath
TCP where the kernel enables it. A new test fails when the build
keeps the old defaults for the query limit or GOMAXPROCS.

Developing the gateway now needs Go 1.27; go commands since 1.21
download it unless GOTOOLCHAIN forbids it. The line names the release
1.27.0 because older go commands such as go1.22.2 look for a
nonexistent "go1.27" toolchain when the line is a bare "go 1.27".
